          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Recall that it is possible to define an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internalization
            of the set of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
            (NNO), in any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian monoidal category
            (a category with finite
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} products
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic"} intuitionistic
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher-order+logic"} higher-order logic
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integer"} integers
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+number"} rationals
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            in any topos with an NNO.
          %p More generally, we can define a real numbers object (RNO) in any category with sufficient structure; RNOs can be defined for many flavors of category, from cartesian monoidal categories up to topoi. Then we can prove that an RNO exists in any topos with an NNO, as well as in some other situations.
          %h2#definition Definition
